  "cut_point": "We are given two sets:\n\n- $ A = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\} $\n- $ B = \\{-1, -2\\} $\n\nA mapping $ f: A \\rightarrow B $ means that each element in $ A $ is mapped to an element in $ B $.\n\nWe are told that **every element in $ B $ is an image of an element in $ A $**. Since $ B $ has only **two elements**, and each of those elements must be mapped to, we must have **exactly two different mappings** from $ A $ to $ B $, one to each element in $ B $.",
  "reflection": {
    "verified_state": [
      "A = {1, 2, 3, 4} and B = {-1, -2}.",
      "A mapping f: A -> B assigns each of the 4 elements of A to one of the 2 elements of B.",
      "The phrase 'every element in B is an image of an element in A' means f is surjective: both -1 and -2 must be used at least once."
    ],
    "error_step": "Since $ B $ has only **two elements**, and each of those elements must be mapped to, we must have **exactly two different mappings** from $ A $ to $ B $, one to each element in $ B $.",
    "error_reason": "This confuses the requirement that both elements of B be hit at least once with a claim that there are only two mappings. Surjectivity allows many assignments as long as neither element of B is missed.",
    "new_direction": "Count all functions from A to B and subtract the two constant functions that miss one element of B."
  }
